Ownership is measured by `git blame` line attribution across the 19 tracked files, which shows who last wrote each line that exists today.

I ignored the 4-space/double-quote reformat commit (`62628c0`) through the repo's `.git-blame-ignore-revs`, and I ignored whitespace changes. I also followed code moved or copied between files. Without those settings, the reformat would have credited most lines to whoever ran it.

| Author | Surviving lines | Share of the top 25 |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 810 | ~41% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 629 | ~32% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | ~5% |
| Jon Church | 72 | ~4% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | ~3% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | ~3% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | ~2% |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 | ~2% |
| Linus Unnebäck | 26 | ~1% |
| Everyone else | under 20 each | the remainder |

- **Two authors dominate:** Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson together own roughly 73% of the code that survives.
- **Limits of the count:** I only listed the top 25 authors, so the shares are approximate. Authors with only a few lines each are not shown. Several people appear under more than one name or email, for example "Efe" and "Efe Genc", and "Alejandro Caravaca Puchades" and "alex94puchades". I didn't merge them, so those people's real totals are slightly higher than shown.
- **Meaning of "owns":** It means the last person to touch a line. Someone who rewrote a line a little still gets credit for it, even if the logic came from someone else.

I can merge the duplicate identities with a `.mailmap`, or break the counts down per file or per directory.

