FINAL ANSWER:
Ownership by surviving lines (`git blame -w -M -C`, ignoring the reformat commit `62628c0` through `.git-blame-ignore-revs`). It covers all 1,997 lines in tracked files.

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 810 | 40.6% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 629 | 31.5% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | 4.8% |
| Jon Church | 72 | 3.6% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | 3.4% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | 2.6% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | 2.2% |
| Everyone else (~38 people) | 228 | 11.4% |

- **Two authors own 72% of the code.** Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson wrote most of what remains.
- **JS files only:** Troy has 676 lines and Douglas 394. Nobody else has more than 30. Most of the other authors' lines are in YAML, Markdown and JSON files, not source code.
- **Commit counts mislead here.** Douglas has more commits (143 against 122 for Troy), but fewer surviving lines. Troy's code has lasted longer.
- **Without the ignore file, blame is wrong.** The reformat commit would credit 293 lines to "Fiona Formatter", which is the reformat commit's author. The ignore file moves those lines back to the people who wrote them.
- **Limit:** blame credits the last person to change a line meaningfully. The ignore file and `-w` skip pure formatting and whitespace changes. A line that was rewritten is credited to the person who rewrote it, not the original author. Within a file, `-M` and `-C` still credit lines that were moved or copied from other files to their original author.

